ning at showdown unless your opponent is bluffing with total air (like A-2 or 8-7).\n\nBut since you're in the lead overall (19,605 chips, up from 17,912 cumulative), preserving stack is valuable.\n\nHowever, **checking behind (0)** is safe and allows you to see if you win without investing more.\n\nRaising (100 or more) would represent strength, but you have no real value — no one will call with a worse hand. So a raise can't succeed unless it folds out a better hand — i.e., a bluff. But against a passive opponent (who has mostly checked/called), bluff-raising is low EV.\n\nGoing all-in (-2) is overly aggressive and likely gets called by better hands.\n\nFolding (-1) throws away equity — even weak, you still have some chance (e.g., if opponent has no Jack, no Queen, no 10, you might win by high card King).\n\nBest move: **Check (0)** to avoid putting more chips in with a weak hand, while still realizing small showdown equity.\n\nReason:  \nChecking preserves chips and avoids bloating the pot with a weak one pair hand with the worst kicker. Given the opponent’s passivity and your poor showdown value, calling or raising gains little. Folding gives up too much equity. Checking is the optimal risk-management play.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
