 600 = 1600 already in). You have 19,150 left. Going all-in risks too much for little gain, especially since you're in the lead overall.\n\nBut note: **you're on a near-nut draw**. If a **Q** hits the river, you win with a straight. If a **10** hits, you get two pair or trips. So **your hand has equity**.\n\nHowever, **you cannot be sure you're ahead now**. Opponent may already have:\n- Two pair (K-J, K-9, J-8)\n- A set (unlikely, but possible if they flopped 88 or JJ)\n- A straight (if they had Q-10)\n\nThus, **value betting makes sense**, but **overbetting risks unnecessary loss**.\n\nBest move: **Small raise (100)** to extract value from weaker pairs or draws, while minimizing risk.\n\nBut wait: **you are first to act post-flop**, and if you raise, you may price yourself out if behind.\n\nAlternatively: **checking keeps the pot manageable and may induce a bluff from the opponent** if they think you’re weak.\n\nGiven that:\n- You raised twice already\n- Board is coordinated\n- Opponent hasn't raised back\n\n→ They may call a small bet with worse, or bluff if you check.\n\nBut since you want to **maximize expected value while protecting your chip lead**, a **small bet of 100** is safe and serves as a probe.\n\nReason:  \nA small raise (100) balances value and caution. It protects against pure bluffs, extracts value from weaker pairs (like 8x or Jx), and doesn’t commit you to the pot. Given your prior aggression, this continuation bet will be credible. It’s larger than minimum allowed (100 is valid), and well under your stack.\n\nAnswer: 100"}
